Story location metadata: overall='a medieval tavern'; starting='inside a wooden‑topped tavern'; setting='The medieval tavern is a rectangular room measuring sixty feet from north to south and forty feet from east to west, surrounded by thick stone walls. The wooden floor creaks underfoot. At the center of the south wall sits the back door, spanning ten feet wide; it has a wooden latch that secures entry.\n\nIn the middle of the room stands a central hearth—a stone fireplace—12 feet across and 6 feet deep, centered on both axes. Adjacent to its north side runs an eight‑foot long counter made of wood, extending four feet westward from the hearth’s edge. The counter is fixed in place and supports the bar area.\n\nAlong the west wall just east of the counter lies a high bar stool for larger patrons; it sits at five feet from the west wall and thirty‑five feet north of the south wall. Behind this stool, directly behind the counter, is a polished wooden table that backs the bar area: six feet long by four feet wide.\n\nBeside the hearth on its east side rests an eight‑foot tall barrel with a three‑foot diameter; it sits between twenty‑six and twenty‑nine feet from the west wall at thirty feet north of the south wall.\n\nPolished tables line each wall. East‑wall tables are six feet long by two feet wide, positioned at ten, twenty, thirty, forty, and fifty feet north of the south wall, spanning from thirty‑two to thirty‑eight feet east of the west wall. West‑wall tables share the same dimensions and sit at twelve, twenty‑four, thirty‑six, forty‑eight, and sixty feet north of the south wall, ranging from four to ten feet east of the west wall. A single table sits on the north wall: six by two feet, centered between fifteen and twenty‑five feet east of the west wall at fifty‑eight feet north.\n\nAbove each polished table hangs a wooden lantern suspended seven feet above floor level; its light radius covers the corresponding table area. The lanterns are fixed fixtures that sup